 # Memory safety and leak checks
 
-CUI uses several independent checks. Passing them means the exercised paths had no reported errors; it does not prove that every C execution is safe. These are development tools, not shipped library dependencies. The current executed backend is Linux/GTK. Windows and macOS verification is deferred.
+CUI uses several independent checks. Passing them means the exercised paths had no reported errors; it does not prove that every C execution is safe. These are development tools, not shipped library dependencies. The safety-tool suite described below has been executed on Linux/GTK. The separate Windows repaint investigation records native Windows test results and their limits; macOS verification is deferred.
 
 ## Run the checks
 
@@ -69,6 +69,53 @@ Two additional Valgrind conditional-value exceptions are restricted to librsvg 2
 
 The Ubuntu reproduction passes all five selected Memcheck workloads and the ASan/UBSan suite (43 tests passed, one window-manager-dependent test skipped). The new populated/empty table weak-reference checks also pass under ASan at normal and 2× scale. Clang static analysis reports no bugs. The older Arch host's missing loader symbols remain an environment limitation; those failed starts are not counted as verification.
 
+## Windows repaint allocation investigation
+
+An experiment reused the existing `WriteableBitmap` for same-size frames instead
+of replacing it on every canvas presentation. Native comparisons did not show a
+material memory reduction, so the experiment was removed. The regression test
+and improved exception diagnostics remain. Linux renderer measurements cannot
+answer the separate Windows memory question.
+
+With a native Windows build and `CUI_BUILD_TESTS=ON`, run:
+
+```powershell
+ctest --test-dir build -C Release -R "winui_memory|winui_contracts" -V
+```
+
+`winui_memory` performs 1,000 canvas updates, changes backing scale from 1 to 2
+and back, changes opacity, waits for presentation work to settle, then tears down
+the app. It logs working set, private commit, process handles and GDI/USER object
+counts. Compare the same workload before and after on the same Windows host.
+Memory values are diagnostic output, not a fixed pass threshold; passing the
+functional assertions alone does not prove stable memory or absence of leaks.
+On 2026-10-15, both variants built with MSVC 19.44 and Windows SDK 10.0.26100
+and ran in the local Windows 11 VM. Both contract and repaint tests returned
+success. At step 400, baseline/fixed private commit was 21.16/20.98 MiB;
+after resizing and settling it was 27.14/27.00 MiB. Peak sampled working set
+was approximately 112 MiB in both. This does **not** demonstrate a material
+memory reduction or reproduce Archaic's reported growth to 300 MB.
+
+Both variants also triggered an access violation observed by the diagnostic
+handler during COM teardown, inside `Microsoft.UI.Xaml.Controls.dll`
+(Windows App Runtime 1.8, package version 8000.994.2142.0). Execution continued
+and the processes returned success; a vectored exception handler sees exceptions
+that may subsequently be handled. These runs are therefore not described as
+exception-free. The diagnostic now reports the faulting module and avoids
+incorrectly labeling every observed exception as unhandled. A normal synced
+Archaic session subsequently reached about 310–320 MiB of total working set and
+200–210 MiB of private commit, with roughly 195–198 MB in Task Manager. Its image
+cache held about 10 MiB, and memory flattened while idle. This is not a reproduction
+of 300 MB in Task Manager or proof of an unbounded leak. Windows heap snapshots
+showed growing allocations through Archaic's SQLite dependency; its connection-pool
+budget was reduced in the application, independently of CUI rendering. In a
+matched nine-chat workload with the same CUI DLL, Archaic's median private commit
+fell from 124.99 to 77.04 MiB. This result measures a specific SQLite configuration
+change and workload; it is not a universal application-memory limit.
+
+The buffer-update mechanism follows Microsoft's
+[WriteableBitmap API](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/windows-app-sdk/api/winrt/microsoft.ui.xaml.media.imaging.writeablebitmap?view=windows-app-sdk-1.8).
+
 ## Evidence and limits
 
 The completed 30-second fuzz runs processed 25,310,636 icon inputs, 2,755,762 model inputs and 12,105,547 layout inputs with no reported sanitizer findings. These are bounded smoke campaigns: model inputs contain at most 32 entries and layouts at most 16 immediate children. Existing deterministic GUI tests cover larger models. Preserve any crashing input and add a focused regression after fixing its cause.
